analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment:

```java
// @@@ If the return value is null, then either the key was not present in the map originally, or the original value associated with the key was null.
```

We need to refer to the method remove(Object key). The method returns V (value). The comment says: If the return value is null, then either the key was not present originally, or the original value associated with the key was null.

We need to express that as an assertion after the method body.

We have observer methods: containsKey(Object key), get(Object key). But after removal, the map may have changed. To refer to original state, we can use \old on methods.

We need to express: (\result == null) => (!\old(this.containsKey(key)) || \old(this.get(key)) == null)

Because if key not present originally, containsKey false. If present, then original value is null. So condition: either not present, or original value null.

Thus assert: assert (\result == null) => (!\old(this.containsKey(key)) || \old(this.get(key)) == null);

Need to use proper syntax: parentheses for implication.

Thus final answer:

```java
assert (\result == null) => (!\old(this.containsKey(key)) || \old(this.get(key)) == null);
```

Check: Use \old(this.containsKey(key)) and \old(this.get(key)). That's fine.

Return type V may be object; comparing to null is okay.

Thus output.assistantfinal<code>assert (\result == null) => (!\old(this.containsKey(key)) || \old(this.get(key)) == null);</code>
